  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 255 PM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Continued dry with mostly sunny conditions expected \nlate week into the weekend. \n\nStrong low pressure (~990mb) remains well offshore this afternoon \nwith high pressure over the centered over the Ohio Valley. Elevated \nN and NNW winds continue, especially near the coast this afternoon. \nWinds will decrease this evening for inland locations but will \nremain breezy near the coast overnight as low pressure deepens and \nbegins to finally lift away to the north and east. Clear skies and \nlighter winds will lead to fairly good radiational cooling tonight \nwith lows in the mid and upper 30s near and west of I-95 and low 40s \nto the east. Immediate coastal locations may stay in the upper 40s \nto low 50s with more mixing expected overnight. Some patchy frost is \npossible tonight, mainly in the sheltered/cooler Piedmont locations. \nNot confident enough in frost coverage for any advisories at this \ntime. \n\nHigh pressure will dominate the local weather pattern late week into \nthe weekend with the low offshore finally moving away by\ntomorrow night. Continued the trend of going a degree or two \nwarmer than the blended guidance for afternoon highs Thursday \nwith most spots in the upper 60s to low 70s. A little more wind \nis expected Thursday night as high pressure builds north of the \nregion. This should help to moderate overnight lows with low to \nmid 40s forecast for inland areas and upper 40s to low 50s near \nthe water. Cooler Friday afternoon with highs ranging from \naround 60 N to the mid and upper 60s S. Friday night looks to be\nthe coolest of the forecast period as ridging expands over the \nregion with light winds and clear skies. Some frost is possible,\nagain mainly in the cooler more sheltered spots in the \nPiedmont. Looking ahead, not much change in the forecast with \nseasonal temperatures and a near zero chance for much needed \nrainfall returning to the region. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Elevated tidal anomalies through Friday with minor \nto locally moderate tidal flooding forecast.\n\nLow pressure lingering offshore with elevated winds and large swells \nare resulting in tidal anomalies around 2 ft, expected to increase a \nbit further over the next 24-36 hours. Coastal Flood Warnings have \nbeen issued for bay-side portions of the MD Eastern Shore as well as \nalong the tidal Potomac. A Coastal Flood Warning for VA Beach also \nremains in effect. Elsewhere, nuisance to minor tidal flooding is \nforecast over the next few high tide cycles. Coastal Flood \nAdvisories continue tonight and Thursday for most areas and have \nbeen extended into Friday, primarily for the western shore of the \nChesapeake Bay and adjacent tidal rivers.",
